                "file": "src/Lending.Application/Views.fs",
                "line": 290,
                "quote": "if was < fine.Amount && now >= fine.Amount then Some(CopyId.value fine.Copy) else None"
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "The oldest-first allocation, which is the core rule, lives in Tier 2 (Accounts.allocate and finesWithPaid). Tier 3 Views then re-derives the paid/unpaid/partial status, and ofPayment re-derives which fines a payment finished, using its own predicates over the allocation results. These copies do not contradict Tier 2, but a status derivation is being decided in the projection layer. No rule is decided only in Tier 3 or 4.",
            "score": 1
          },
          "ARC-REV-02": {
            "evidence": [],
            "rationale": "The change introduces no new effects. The allocation is pure Tier 2 code, the views are pure functions, and the CLI only formats strings. Nothing in Tier 4 presentation calls Tier 2, and no port is bypassed.",
            "score": 2
          },
          "ARC-REV-03": {
            "evidence": [
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/Views.fs",
                "line": 270,
                "quote": "if paid >= fine.Amount then \"paid\""
              },
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/Views.fs",
                "line": 290,
                "quote": "was < fine.Amount && now >= fine.Amount"
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "The allocation itself has one definition, `Accounts.allocate`, which both `finesWithPaid` and `ofPayment` use. The 'fine fully paid' predicate is written out separately in two places in Views, in the status text and in the settled computation. The two copies agree, so this is a restated fact with no drift today.",
            "score": 1
          }
        },
        "2": {
